From 0e1e7e038ddd6a14ae3a8751687494e799a73718 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 06:21:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] Give this service the pipeline the other two have had for
 months
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Until now every change here reached production by hand: a patch file mailed
out, uploaded to the box, `git am`, `docker compose build`. That is how the
fence-guard fix sat undeployed, and how a placeholder once got pasted into
AI_PRIMARY_MODEL in two .env files including the ERP's. The frontend and
backend have had CI-gated deploys with rollback for months; this repo was the
exception, and the exception was the expensive part.

Three workflows, same shape as the siblings so a reader who knows one knows
all three:

  ci.yml        eslint + the 44-test security suite + an advisory npm audit
  deploy.yml    gated on CI, runs on the VPS, health-checks afterwards
  rollback.yml  a specific SHA, straight to the box, confirmation required

Deploy runs on a self-hosted runner rather than reaching in over SSH — the
siblings had to remove a four-attempt retry ladder to get there, and this repo
starts where they ended up. docs/self-hosted-runner.md covers the setup and,
more importantly, what it costs: the runner is a process on the production
box, so anyone who can merge a workflow here can run code there.

deploy.yml does not stop at `docker compose up -d`. config.js is fail-fast by
design, so a bad AI_API_KEY or a forbidden secret name makes the container
exit AFTER a perfectly successful `up -d` — the deploy would report green
while nothing served. It polls /health for ten seconds and fails the job with
the container's logs if nothing answers.

rollback.yml notes the one coupling worth checking: the panel calls
/ai/client-agent/chat/stream, so rolling back past the commit that added it
would leave the deployed UI calling a route that no longer exists.
